WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.200
Hi! We have this question on our Quiz, where some&nbsp;
of you asked why it’s the convolutional neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.880
network’s depth that increases the receptive&nbsp;
field, or context window in other words.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:14.400
This is a case where a picture is&nbsp;
worth more than a YouTube comment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.800
so here is the visualized explanation:

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.200
Increasing the kernel size in a CNN&nbsp;
also increases the context window,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.480
since the output is directly&nbsp;
informed by more pixels.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.080
But this would be a very costly&nbsp;
way to do it since the parameter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.800
number scales quadratically with kernel size.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.360
Having more of these kernels (or filters),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.040
does help with feature diversity, but has&nbsp;
nothing to do with the receptive field.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.400
But having a deep CNN with one&nbsp;
convolutional layer one after the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.000
greatly increases the receptive field.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.720
In a nutshell, if the output after the&nbsp;
first layer is this and was informed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.560
by the whole region of 4 pixels,
then the next convolution layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:57.840
aggregates again 4 pixels, but now one of&nbsp;
them is already informed by four others!

00:00:58.960 --> 00:00:59.760
Okay, bye!

